# {{ title }}

Two projects that together publish one implementation guide from one DHIS2
instance:

| Directory | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `{{ registry_root }}/` | A **package**. It publishes the organisation unit hierarchy and nothing else. |
| `{{ guide_root }}/` | A **guide**. It publishes the forms, and depends on the package for its units. |

The guide writes no `Organization` and no `Location` of its own. It references
every unit by its URL under the package's canonical,
`{{ canonical }}/{{ registry_root }}`, and declares the package as a dependency.

## The whole thing

```bash
make setup        # once per machine: the SUSHI + IG publisher docker image
make generate     # read the instance, write both projects' sources
make build        # build the registry, install it, build the guide
make serve        # serve the guide with the capture UI
```

`make help` lists every target; `make toolchain` prints which `d2w` each project
resolves, which is the first thing to check if a `fhir.toml` key reads as
unknown.

## The build order is not a preference

`make build` builds the registry first, then installs its `package.tgz` into the
publisher's shared package cache, then builds the guide. SUSHI and the IG
publisher resolve a declared dependency from that cache and nowhere else, so a
guide built before the registry has a dependency it cannot find.

To build the guide against a package that did not come from the sibling
checkout, name the archive:

```bash
cd {{ guide_root }} && make build REGISTRY_TGZ=/path/to/package.tgz
```

## Sizing the registry

The registry is usually the larger build by a wide margin - one resource per
organisation unit, each validated and rendered. Cap its depth in
`{{ registry_root }}/fhir.toml` while you are finding your feet:

```toml
[generate.organisation_units]
max_level = 4
```

Keep the guide's own `[generate.organisation_units]` saying the same thing: it
decides which units the forms may refer to, and `make check` reports every
reference the package does not publish.

The two builds never share a container, which is the point of the split: the
registry's depth is a dial on the registry project, not on the guide. Each sizes
the publisher's heap from this machine's docker VM, so neither needs telling;
`make build JAVA_HEAP=8g` overrides both at once when you want to say.

## Reading further

The reference for this shape is **Publish the registry as a package** in the
dhis2w FHIR documentation. Every option with its default is catalogued in
`fhir.example.toml` inside each project, regenerated by `d2w fhir init --refresh`.
